28:["$","li","09-comparing-and-why-it-matters",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#09-comparing-and-why-it-matters","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"09"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Comparing them, and why engineers care"}]]}]}]
29:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-the-scheduling-problem",{"id":"01-the-scheduling-problem","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The scheduling problem: too many threads, too few CPUs"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Start with the uncomfortable arithmetic. A modern machine might have 8 CPU cores and 400 runnable threads. Only 8 can physically execute at any given instant; the other 392 are ready and willing but simply have nowhere to run. The scheduler is the part of the kernel that resolves this mismatch: from the pool of threads that are ready to run, it repeatedly chooses which ones actually get a core, and for how long. Do it well and every program feels responsive and the machine stays busy; do it badly and the box feels sluggish even when the CPU is nearly idle."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The key insight that makes scheduling both possible and necessary is that programs almost never use the CPU continuously. A process runs for a little while, computing, comparing, looping, and then stops to wait for something slow: a disk read, a network reply, a key press. That pause is not wasted only if the scheduler immediately hands the freed CPU to someone else. So a program’s life is really a repeating cycle: a CPU burst (a stretch of pure computation), then an I/O burst (a stretch of waiting), then another CPU burst, and so on until it exits."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Ready queue"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The set of threads that are loaded, runnable, and waiting only for a CPU, not blocked on I/O, not sleeping. The scheduler picks its next victim from here."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CPU burst"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A continuous stretch during which a thread wants the CPU and does nothing but compute."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"I/O burst"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A stretch during which the thread is blocked waiting for a device (disk, network, terminal) and needs no CPU at all."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CPU–I/O burst cycle"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The lifelong alternation between CPU bursts and I/O bursts that characterises essentially every real program."}]]}]]}],"$L2b","$L2c","$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}]]}],"$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32","$L33","$L34","$L35","$L36"]}]
2a:["$","$L37",null,{"prev":{"slug":"context-switching","title":"Context Switching & the Dispatcher","kind":"$undefined","number":10},"next":{"slug":"cpu-scheduling-real","title":"CPU Scheduling II: Real Schedulers","kind":"$undefined","number":12}}]
38:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2b:["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This cycle lets us sort programs into two rough temperaments, and the distinction drives almost every scheduling decision. A CPU-bound program has long CPU bursts and rare I/O, think of video encoding, a compiler optimiser, or a number-crunching simulation; it wants the CPU for as long as it can get it. An I/O-bound program has short CPU bursts punctuated by frequent, long waits, think of a text editor, a database serving small queries, or a web server; it needs the CPU only briefly but wants it right away, the instant its data arrives."}]
2c:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CPU-bound"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Spends most of its time computing; long CPU bursts, little I/O. Throughput matters more than snappiness."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"I/O-bound"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Spends most of its time waiting on devices; short, frequent CPU bursts. Fast response matters most."}]]}]]}]
2d:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why this split matters",": "]}],"A good scheduler favours I/O-bound work with the CPU briefly and often. Handing an I/O-bound thread the core the moment it wakes keeps the slow devices busy and the interface responsive, while the CPU-bound threads soak up whatever cycles are left. Almost every clever policy in this chapter is, at heart, a way to detect and reward this behaviour."]}]]}]
2e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F01-the-scheduling-problem.jpg?v=66bd51a95fa88f3c5d164bbe2f95f68b","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a ready queue drawn as a horizontal row of process boxes (P1 P2 P3 P4) feeding into a single CPU box on the right; a \"scheduler\" label with an arrow picks one box to admit while the rest wait. Below, a timeline of one process shown as alternating shaded blocks labelled \"CPU burst\" and \"I/O burst (waiting on disk/network)\", illustrating the compute-then-wait cycle."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2f:["$","section","02-when-scheduling-happens",{"id":"02-when-scheduling-happens","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"When scheduling happens: preemptive vs non-preemptive"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The scheduler does not run constantly, it runs at specific moments, when something happens that might make the current choice of running thread wrong. There are exactly four such moments in the life of a running thread, and knowing them precisely is what lets you reason about any scheduling policy."}],["$","ul","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"1. A running thread blocks, it makes an I/O request or waits on something, and goes from running to waiting. The CPU is now free and must be given to someone."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"2. A running thread is interrupted, typically by the hardware timer, and is pushed from running back to ready even though it could still compute. This is the decisive one."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"3. A waiting thread’s I/O completes, so it moves from waiting to ready. It may now deserve the CPU more than whoever is running."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"4. A running thread terminates. The CPU is free and a new thread must be selected."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Cases 1 and 4 are unavoidable: the running thread has given up the CPU on its own, so the scheduler simply must pick a replacement, there is no choice about whether to schedule, only about whom. Cases 2 and 3 are where the real policy decision lives, because the current thread could keep running but the OS is choosing whether to take the CPU away from it. A scheduler that reschedules only at 1 and 4 is non-preemptive (or cooperative); one that also acts at 2 and 3 is preemptive."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Non-preemptive (cooperative)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Once a thread has the CPU it keeps it until it blocks or exits. The scheduler never forcibly interrupts a running thread. Simple, but one thread can hog the CPU forever."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":["$L39","$L3a"]}],"$L3b"]}],"$L3c","$L3d"]}],"$L3e"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","03-criteria-and-metrics",{"id":"03-criteria-and-metrics","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Scheduling criteria: what \"better\" even means"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"To compare scheduling policies we need numbers, not opinions. There is a standard vocabulary of metrics, and the crucial thing to grasp is that no single policy can maximise all of them at once, they pull against each other. A policy that is superb for one metric is often mediocre for another, so choosing a scheduler is really choosing which metric you care about most for your workload."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CPU utilisation"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The fraction of time the CPU is doing useful work rather than sitting idle. We want it high, ideally the CPU is never idle while any thread is ready to run."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Throughput"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The number of processes completed per unit time. A batch system that finishes 100 jobs an hour has higher throughput than one that finishes 60."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Turnaround time"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"For one process, the total elapsed time from when it arrives to when it finishes, completion time minus arrival time. It counts everything: running, waiting in the ready queue, and blocked on I/O."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Waiting time"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The total time a process spends sitting in the ready queue, ready to run but not running. This is the part a scheduler can actually reduce; it cannot shorten the work itself."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Response time"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The time from a process’s arrival to the first moment it starts running, how long until it produces its first sign of life. This is the metric interactive users feel most directly."}]]}],["$","div","5",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Fairness"}],"$L3f"]}]]}],"$L40","$L41","$L42"]}],"$L43"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","04-fcfs",{"id":"04-fcfs","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"FCFS: First-Come, First-Served"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The simplest possible policy is the one at every supermarket till: serve threads in the order they arrive, and let each run to completion before starting the next. First-Come, First-Served is non-preemptive, once a thread has the CPU it keeps it until its CPU burst ends, and the ready queue is just a plain FIFO. It is trivial to implement and impossible to accuse of playing favourites. It is also, as we will see, quietly terrible for average performance."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Take three processes that all arrive at time 0, in the order P1, P2, P3, with the CPU-burst lengths shown. FCFS runs them back to back in arrival order."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"FCFS, worked example (all arrive at t = 0, order P1, P2, P3)"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Process   Burst\n  P1        24\n  P2         3\n  P3         3\n\nGantt chart:\n  | P1 (0–24) | P2 (24–27) | P3 (27–30) |\n\nProcess   Turnaround   Waiting\n  P1          24           0\n  P2          27          24\n  P3          30          27\n  ------------------------------\n  Average    27.0        17.0"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"An average waiting time of 17 is dreadful, and it is entirely P1’s fault. P2 and P3 each need only 3 units of CPU, but because they had the bad luck to queue behind a 24-unit job, they wait 24 and 27 units respectively before they even start. The two quick jobs are held hostage by the one slow one."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Now watch what happens if the very same processes simply arrive in a different order: P2, P3, P1, with nothing else changed."}],["$","figure","5",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"FCFS, same processes, arrival order P2, P3, P1"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Gantt chart:\n  | P2 (0–3) | P3 (3–6) | P1 (6–30) |\n\nProcess   Turnaround   Waiting\n  P2           3           0\n  P3           6           3\n  P1          30           6\n  ------------------------------\n  Average    13.0         3.0"}]}]]}],"$L44"]}],"$L45"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","05-sjf",{"id":"05-sjf","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"SJF: Shortest Job First"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"If short jobs stuck behind long ones is the disease, the obvious cure is to run the shortest job first. Shortest Job First picks, from all the ready processes, the one with the smallest next CPU burst, and (in this non-preemptive version) runs it to completion before choosing again. Intuitively this gets the little jobs out of the way fast, and that intuition turns out to be provably correct."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Take four processes arriving together at time 0. SJF sorts them by burst length and runs them shortest first: P4, then P1, then P3, then P2."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"SJF (non-preemptive), worked example (all arrive at t = 0)"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Process   Burst\n  P1         6\n  P2         8\n  P3         7\n  P4         3\n\nRun order (shortest burst first): P4, P1, P3, P2\n\nGantt chart:\n  | P4 (0–3) | P1 (3–9) | P3 (9–16) | P2 (16–24) |\n\nProcess   Turnaround   Waiting\n  P4           3           0\n  P1           9           3\n  P3          16           9\n  P2          24          16\n  ------------------------------\n  Average    13.0         7.0"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"For comparison, plain FCFS on the same four processes in arrival order (P1, P2, P3, P4) yields an average waiting time of 10.25. SJF’s 7.0 is a genuine improvement, and this is no accident: SJF is provably optimal for average waiting time. Given a fixed set of jobs available at the same moment, no other scheduling order can produce a smaller average wait. The proof is a simple exchange argument, if a longer job ever runs before a shorter one, swapping them lowers the total waiting time, but the result is powerful and worth remembering."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The fatal flaw",": "]}],"SJF is optimal, and yet no real CPU scheduler can use it as stated, for one devastating reason: it requires knowing the length of each job’s next CPU burst before running it, and the OS simply cannot see the future. A process does not announce \"my next burst is 6 milliseconds\". So SJF is unimplementable in its pure form."]}]]}],"$L46","$L47"]}],"$L48"]}]]}]
33:["$","section","06-srtf",{"id":"06-srtf","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"SRTF: Shortest Remaining Time First (preemptive SJF)"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"SJF as described only chooses when the CPU is already free. But what if a very short job arrives while a long one is halfway through running? The preemptive version, Shortest Remaining Time First, answers boldly: at every moment, including the instant a new process arrives, pick the process whose remaining CPU time is smallest, preempting the running one if the newcomer is shorter. It is SJF with the freedom to change its mind mid-run."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is our first example with staggered arrival times, so read the trace carefully. Each time a process arrives, we compare its burst against the remaining time of whoever is running."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"SRTF, worked example (processes arrive at different times)"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Process   Arrival   Burst\n  P1         0         8\n  P2         1         4\n  P3         2         9\n  P4         3         5\n\nTrace of decisions:\n  t=0  only P1 ready            -> run P1\n  t=1  P2 arrives: 4 < 7 rem    -> preempt, run P2\n  t=5  P2 done; ready P1=7 P3=9 P4=5 -> run P4 (5 smallest)\n  t=10 P4 done; ready P1=7 P3=9 -> run P1\n  t=17 P1 done; only P3 left    -> run P3\n  t=26 P3 done\n\nGantt chart:\n  | P1 (0–1) | P2 (1–5) | P4 (5–10) | P1 (10–17) | P3 (17–26) |\n\nProcess   Completion   Turnaround   Waiting\n  P1          17            17          9\n  P2           5             4          0\n  P3          26            24         15\n  P4          10             7          2\n  --------------------------------------------\n  Average                  13.0        6.5"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Notice how P1 is torn into two pieces: it runs for a single unit, gets preempted the moment the shorter P2 arrives, and only resumes much later once every shorter job is out of the way. That is preemption made visible on the Gantt chart. The payoff is real: SRTF drives the average waiting time down to 6.5, lower than non-preemptive SJF could manage on staggered arrivals, because a freshly arrived short job never has to wait behind the tail of a long one."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],"$L49"]}]]}],"$L4a"]}]]}]
34:["$","section","07-round-robin",{"id":"07-round-robin","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Round Robin: taking turns"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Round Robin abandons the search for the \"shortest\" job entirely and optimises for a different goal: fairness and responsiveness. It gives every ready thread a small fixed slice of CPU time, the time quantum (or time slice), and cycles through them in turn. When a thread’s quantum expires, the timer interrupt preempts it, and it goes to the back of the ready queue while the next thread gets its slice. The ready queue behaves like a circle: everyone gets a turn, then everyone gets another. It is essentially preemptive FCFS with a stopwatch."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here are the same three processes from the FCFS example, now under Round Robin with a quantum of 4. Watch how the long P1 no longer monopolises the CPU."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Round Robin, worked example (quantum = 4, all arrive at t = 0)"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Process   Burst\n  P1        24\n  P2         3\n  P3         3\n\nSlices: P1(4) P2(3,done) P3(3,done) then P1 runs out its rest\n\nGantt chart:\n  | P1 (0–4) | P2 (4–7) | P3 (7–10) | P1 (10–30) |\n\nProcess   Turnaround   Waiting   Response\n  P1          30           6          0\n  P2           7           4          4\n  P3          10           7          7\n  --------------------------------------------\n  Average    15.7         5.7        3.7"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Compare this with the FCFS run on the identical processes. There, P2 and P3 did not get the CPU until times 24 and 27; here they start at 4 and 7 and both finish by time 10. The average waiting time also drops (5.7 versus 17). Round Robin will rarely beat SJF on raw average waiting time, but it wins decisively on response time and fairness, and unlike SJF it needs no knowledge of the future and cannot starve anyone, because every thread is guaranteed the CPU within one full cycle of the queue."}],["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Everything hinges on the size of the quantum, and it is a genuine trade-off with two failure modes at the extremes."}],["$","ul","5",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Quantum too large: if the slice is longer than most CPU bursts, threads finish or block before their quantum ever expires, so preemption almost never fires, and Round Robin quietly degenerates into plain FCFS, convoy effect and all."}]]}],"$L4b","$L4c"]}],"$L4d"]}],"$L4e"]}]]}]
35:["$","section","08-priority",{"id":"08-priority","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Priority scheduling & the starvation problem"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Priority scheduling generalises the idea behind SJF: attach a priority number to every process and always run the highest-priority ready process. In fact SJF is just priority scheduling where the priority is the inverse of the predicted burst length. Priorities can come from many places, how important the user is, how much the process paid, how time-critical its work is, and the scheduler need not care where they came from, only how to compare them. (By convention we will treat a smaller number as a higher priority, as most Unix systems do.)"}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Priority scheduling comes in the same two flavours we have now seen twice. A non-preemptive version picks the highest-priority job whenever the CPU is free and lets it finish its burst; a preemptive version immediately snatches the CPU away whenever a higher-priority process becomes ready. Here is a non-preemptive worked example with all processes present at time 0."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Priority scheduling (non-preemptive), smaller number = higher priority"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Process   Burst   Priority\n  P1        10        3\n  P2         1        1\n  P3         2        4\n  P4         1        5\n  P5         5        2\n\nRun order (highest priority first): P2, P5, P1, P3, P4\n\nGantt chart:\n  | P2 (0–1) | P5 (1–6) | P1 (6–16) | P3 (16–18) | P4 (18–19) |\n\nProcess   Turnaround   Waiting\n  P1          16           6\n  P2           1           0\n  P3          18          16\n  P4          19          18\n  P5           6           1\n  ------------------------------\n  Average    12.0         8.2"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Priority scheduling is enormously flexible, it is how a system expresses that some work matters more than other work, but it carries the same danger SJF did, and here it has a name and a famous cautionary tale. If high-priority processes keep arriving, a low-priority process may sit in the ready queue indefinitely, never quite reaching the front. This is starvation (or indefinite blocking)."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":["$L4f","The classic story: when an IBM 7094 at MIT was shut down in 1973, operators reportedly found a low-priority job that had been submitted in 1967 and had never run, starved for six years. Whether the details are exact or not, it captures the failure mode perfectly: a pure priority scheduler can leave the unlucky waiting effectively forever."]}]]}],"$L50","$L51"]}],"$L52"]}]]}]
36:["$","section","09-comparing-and-why-it-matters",{"id":"09-comparing-and-why-it-matters","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"09"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Comparing them, and why engineers care"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Lay the five algorithms side by side and their personalities snap into focus. None is best; each trades one virtue for another, and the right choice depends entirely on what your workload values."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"The five basic algorithms at a glance"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"text"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"Algorithm  Preempt?  Fairness  Starvation   Overhead   Needs to know\n---------  --------  --------  ----------   --------   -------------\nFCFS         no       poor        no          low        nothing\nSJF          no       poor       yes(long)    low        next burst*\nSRTF        yes       poor       yes(long)    medium     remaining*\nRound Robin yes       good        no          med/high   nothing\nPriority    either   depends    yes->aging    low/med    priorities\n\n* burst length cannot be known in advance, must be estimated."}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Read the table as a set of engineering choices, not as trivia. If you know nothing about your jobs and just want simplicity, FCFS is honest but risks the convoy effect. If you want the mathematically lowest average wait and can estimate burst lengths, SJF/SRTF are optimal but fragile and prone to starvation. If you care about responsiveness and fairness above all, the interactive case: Round Robin is the workhorse. And if some work genuinely matters more than other work, priority scheduling expresses that directly, provided you add aging so the small jobs are not left to rot."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Batch workloads"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Long, throughput-oriented jobs where no human is waiting, data pipelines, builds, scientific runs. Favour policies that maximise throughput and keep the CPU saturated (SJF-like ideas, big quanta)."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Interactive workloads"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Short, latency-sensitive work where a person or client is waiting on every keystroke or request, editors, shells, web servers. Favour low response time and fairness (Round Robin, priority boosts for I/O-bound threads)."}]]}]]}],"$L53","$L54"]}],"$L55"]}]]}]
39:["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Preemptive"}]
3a:["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The OS can forcibly take the CPU from a running thread, because its time slice expired or a higher-priority thread became ready, and give it to another. Essential for responsiveness and fairness."}]
3b:["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Timer interrupt"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A hardware timer the kernel programs to fire periodically (say every few milliseconds). Its interrupt hands control to the kernel, which is what makes preemption at decision point 2 physically possible."}]]}]
3c:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Preemption is not something the software can wish into existence, it needs hardware help. Before handing the CPU to a user thread, the kernel arms a timer to fire after a set interval. When the timer’s interrupt arrives, the CPU traps into the kernel regardless of what the user thread was doing, and the scheduler gets to run. Without that timer, a buggy or greedy thread with an infinite loop would keep the core forever and nothing else would ever run. The timer interrupt is the leash that makes true multitasking possible."}]
3d:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Engineer’s note",": "]}],"Early consumer systems, classic Mac OS and Windows 3.x, were cooperative: a program kept the CPU until it politely yielded, so one frozen app froze the whole machine. Modern desktop, server, and mobile operating systems are all preemptive precisely so that no single misbehaving process can lock everyone else out. When you cannot understand how a runaway thread got interrupted, the answer is almost always the timer."]}]]}]
3e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F02-when-scheduling-happens.jpg?v=0995e046f07fdc009ea25c14e7d13581","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a state-transition sketch of a process with the four scheduling decision points numbered, (1) running → waiting (blocks on I/O), (2) running → ready (timer interrupt / preemption), (3) waiting → ready (I/O completes), (4) running → terminated (exit). A hardware timer icon with a downward \"tick\" arrow triggers point 2. A side box contrasts \"non-preemptive: only 1 and 4\" with \"preemptive: all four\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3f:["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Every thread makes reasonable progress and none is starved indefinitely. Not a single number, but a property every practical scheduler must protect."}]
40:["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Two of these are easy to confuse, and the difference is worth burning in. Waiting time is the sum of all the time a process spends queued but not running, it can accumulate across many separate turns. Response time is only about the very first turn: how long you waited before anything happened at all. A batch scheduler might give you a fine average waiting time yet a terrible response time; an interactive scheduler does the opposite, jumping to serve you fast even if your total waiting across the whole job is larger."}]
41:["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The central tension",": "]}],"Optimising for throughput (finish whole jobs efficiently) tends to hurt response time (start every job quickly), and vice-versa. A video encoder wants long uninterrupted CPU bursts; a text editor wants the CPU the instant you press a key. One machine runs both. The scheduler’s real job is not to win one metric but to strike a defensible balance across a mixed workload, and to never let any thread starve while doing it."]}]]}]
42:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Throughout the worked examples below we will report average waiting time and average turnaround time, because they are the easiest to compute by hand and expose each algorithm’s character most clearly. Keep response time and fairness in the back of your mind, though, they are exactly the metrics that eventually kill the \"optimal\" algorithm and force us toward Round Robin."}]
43:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F03-criteria-and-metrics.jpg?v=e20958c7c1d6d479826a6c234914f160","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a single process timeline annotated to show where each metric is measured, arrival time marked with an arrow, the gaps where it sits in the ready queue bracketed as \"waiting time\", first moment it runs marked \"response time\", and the full span from arrival to completion bracketed as \"turnaround time\". Beside it a small tug-of-war sketch labelled \"throughput vs response time\" and \"utilisation vs fairness\" showing the metrics pulling against each other."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
44:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The convoy effect",": "]}],"Average waiting fell from 17 to 3 with no change but ordering. This is the convoy effect: when one long CPU-bound process holds the CPU, a convoy of short (often I/O-bound) processes piles up behind it, and every slow device they would have kept busy goes idle. Under FCFS a single hog wrecks the whole system’s responsiveness, and there is nothing FCFS can do about it, it never preempts. That flaw is exactly what the next algorithm sets out to fix."]}]]}]
45:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F04-fcfs.jpg?v=66290c1b0c5456ababbd77cdbb746558","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a single-file queue of process boxes entering a CPU strictly in arrival order (a supermarket-till metaphor sketched beside it). Below, a Gantt bar with one very long P1 block followed by two tiny P2 and P3 blocks, and a caption \"convoy effect: short jobs stuck behind one long one\". A second smaller Gantt shows the reordered case with much shorter waits."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
46:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"There are two escapes, and both matter downstream. First, we can estimate the next burst by assuming it resembles recent ones, schedulers keep an exponentially weighted moving average of a thread’s past CPU bursts and treat that as the predicted length. Second, in special settings like batch systems, users can supply an estimated run time up front (and are penalised if they guess badly). Neither is perfect, which is why SJF lives on as an ideal to approximate rather than a policy to run verbatim."}]
47:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Starvation",": "]}],"SJF has a second problem even if you could predict bursts: in a busy system with a steady stream of short jobs, a long job may never be the shortest and so may never run, it starves. Any policy that ranks jobs by some property has to answer \"what stops the unlucky ones from waiting forever?\", a question we finally answer with aging in the priority section."]}]]}]
48:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F05-sjf.jpg?v=1a8eab383456137643d788f95e477c87","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: four process boxes labelled with their burst lengths being sorted shortest-to-longest before entering the CPU, with a \"pick the smallest next\" arrow. A Gantt bar shows P4(3), P1(6), P3(7), P2(8) in ascending order. Two warning bubbles read \"can’t know the burst in advance\" and \"long jobs may starve\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
49:["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Same virtues, same vices, plus one",": "]}],"SRTF is optimal for average waiting time among preemptive policies, but it inherits SJF’s fatal flaw (you still cannot know remaining bursts in advance) and its starvation problem, now sharper: a long job can be preempted again and again by an endless trickle of short arrivals and may take a very long time to finish. It also adds overhead, every arrival is a potential context switch. Elegant on paper, awkward in practice."]}]
4a:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F06-srtf.jpg?v=db051d5a366f5f1a2d0ee7ef0c092971","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a timeline with staggered arrival arrows for P1, P2, P3, P4 at different times. At the moment P2 arrives, an arrow shows the CPU being snatched away from P1 (labelled \"preempt: 4 < 7 remaining\"). The Gantt bar shows P1 split into two separate blocks with P2 and P4 running in between, visually demonstrating preemption."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4b:["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Quantum too small: every slice is over almost as soon as it starts, so the CPU spends a large fraction of its time context-switching instead of doing useful work. The overhead of switching dominates and throughput collapses."}]]}]
4c:["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Just right: a common rule of thumb is to pick a quantum long enough that roughly 80% of CPU bursts finish within a single slice, while keeping context-switch overhead to a small percentage. Typical real values are in the 10–100 millisecond range."}]]}]
4d:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The overhead nobody sees on paper",": "]}],"Our Gantt charts pretend a context switch is free, but it never is, saving and restoring registers, flushing pipelines, and disturbing the caches all cost real time. That hidden cost is precisely why you cannot just shrink the quantum toward zero to make the system maximally fair. Fairness and overhead pull against each other, and the quantum is the knob that balances them."]}]]}]
4e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F07-round-robin.jpg?v=b36c4627a482e001af81ab10db25c971","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: the ready queue drawn as a circle of process boxes with the CPU at the top; each process gets one \"time quantum\" slice then is sent to the back of the circle (a curved arrow). A quantum dial in the corner shows a slider from \"too small = all overhead\" through \"just right\" to \"too big = becomes FCFS\". A Gantt bar shows P1, P2, P3 interleaved in fixed-size slices."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
4f:["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The MIT legend",": "]}]
50:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The standard fix is aging: gradually raise the priority of any process that has been waiting a long time. A job that starts unimportant slowly climbs until, eventually, it becomes the highest-priority thing in the queue and is guaranteed to run. Aging costs almost nothing and dissolves starvation completely, which is why essentially every real priority-based scheduler includes some form of it. Keep this pattern in mind: \"rank by something, but boost whoever has waited too long\", because it reappears constantly in production schedulers and even in application-level job queues."}]
51:["$","dl","6",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Static priority"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Assigned once and never changed for the life of the process (e.g. a fixed importance level)."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Dynamic priority"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Adjusted by the scheduler over time based on behaviour, lowered for CPU hogs, raised for waiters (aging)."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Starvation"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A ready process that is perpetually passed over and may never run."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Aging"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Steadily increasing a waiting process’s priority so that it is eventually guaranteed to be scheduled."}]]}]]}]
52:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F08-priority.jpg?v=b81b18033d031c0d9bd5aa1c7607eb19","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a ready queue where each process box carries a priority number, and the scheduler picks the highest-priority box (a crown icon on the smallest number). A Gantt bar shows P2, P5, P1, P3, P4 in priority order. Below, a low-priority job stuck at the bottom for a long time (labelled \"starving\"), with an \"aging\" arrow slowly raising its priority number until it finally runs."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
53:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is exactly why these decades-old algorithms still matter to you as a working engineer. The moment you configure a thread pool, tune a message-queue worker, set nice values on a Linux box, choose a Kubernetes pod priority, or design a job runner that must not starve small tasks behind big ones, you are making a scheduling decision, and you are choosing, implicitly, among FCFS, SJF, Round Robin, and priority with aging. The vocabulary from this chapter turns those choices from guesswork into reasoning."}]
54:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["What is next",": "]}],"Real operating systems do not pick one of these and stop; they blend them. Interactive threads should feel like Round Robin, background threads should behave like a batch scheduler, and the OS must sort them out automatically without being told which is which. The next chapter builds exactly that: multilevel feedback queues that learn a thread’s behaviour, and the modern schedulers: Linux’s CFS and its successor, that put every idea from this chapter to work at once."]}]]}]
55:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch11%2F09-comparing-and-why-it-matters.jpg?v=5e4afe12e22e90fdee0df10108a47838","alt":"Handwritten A4 comparison table with algorithms as rows (FCFS, SJF, SRTF, Round Robin, Priority) and columns for preemptive?, fairness, starvation risk, overhead, and knowledge needed. Ticks, crosses and short notes fill each cell. Below the table, two labelled buckets: \"batch / throughput\" and \"interactive / response\", with arrows sorting the algorithms into them, and a forward arrow to \"real schedulers: MLFQ, Linux CFS →\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
